[
    {
        "content": "<p>Found an interesting case where my kernel diverged from the official one / nanoda, but I couldn't see a reason for the official behavior. The Lean kernel doesn't accept mutual inductive blocks with parts living in different universes. This might sound \"obviously reasonable\" because if they're genuinely mutually inductive then they must be in the same universe, or else they're actually linearizable and don't need a mutual block. But consider constructions like:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">mutual</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"kn\">inductive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">fromB</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">fromC</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"kn\">inductive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">fromA</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"kn\">inductive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">fromA</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"kn\">end</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>The Lean elaborator rejects this, but you can do <code>addDecl</code> and then you get <code>error: (kernel) mutually inductive types must live in the same universe</code>. The Prop living in between B and C makes this, as far as I understand, safe. On the other hand: I can't find any way to flatten this, to state it in CIC without mutuals. An even weaker variant would be</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">mutual</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"kn\">inductive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">base</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">fromH</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"kn\">inductive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">mk</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"kn\">end</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>which is also rejected and I really can't see a \"reason\" why, except for that it can't be stated in CIC-without-mutuals.</p>\n<p>I ask this because it came up in my own development. Currently, it seems like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The official Lean kernel deliberately rejects this, it's been there basically <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/commit/c3c6c4afc3ba6e9361bcc296fcef11f95a26a51c\">since forever</a>, but I can't find any discussion</li>\n<li>Lean4Lean copies this behavior</li>\n<li>Nanoda has an <code>assert! self.ctx.eq_antisymm(codom_level, st.block_codom.unwrap())</code> and panics</li>\n</ul>\n<p>I also tried to do some literature searching and couldn't find really any discussion!</p>",
        "id": 619318079,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Meiburg",
        "timestamp": 1787758844
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If these types of questions should be put in another thread, let me know; they're just coming out of me trying to build a sufficiently different entry for the arena, and I thought they might make valuable test cases, which is why I'm asking here :)</p>",
        "id": 619318296,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Meiburg",
        "timestamp": 1787758900
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>What does lean-inductive-model do to this?</p>",
        "id": 619318623,
        "sender_full_name": "Jason Rute",
        "timestamp": 1787758971
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It agrees with the others. <code>InductiveModels/Mutual.lean</code> reads every member's resultant sort, takes member 0's as the sort, and requires all others to agree. <a href=\"https://github.com/nomeata/lean-inductive-models/blob/9f12a5c09266452d8114b40f2c3d3944ff48f124/src/InductiveModels/Mutual.lean#L307\">Link</a></p>",
        "id": 619321306,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Meiburg",
        "timestamp": 1787759625
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>One reason this restriction might exist is that one way to justify mutually inductive types is because it can be reduced to an indexed inductive type (in your first example indexed over any type with 3 elements). This is only possible if the types live in the same universes (or at the very least, it will be significantly more complicated otherwise).</p>\n<p>Another reason is that it might be more difficult to state the elimination principle when the types have different universes. When does a mutually inductive predicate have singleton elimination (a.k.a. large elimination, i.e. the motive of the recursor lives in an arbitrary universe, not just Prop) if part of the mutual types are not in <code>Prop</code>? While this might not be a super hard question, it is not immediately obvious, and it is easy to make a mistake when answering such questions, and accidentally introduce an inconsistency in the kernel (this has happened to Rocq with reasonable-looking extensions to their termination checker). So the conservative answer is to just have a restriction like this, where the semantics is well-understood.</p>",
        "id": 619322341,
        "sender_full_name": "Floris van Doorn",
        "timestamp": 1787759876
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>\"The semantics aren't well understood\" definitely makes sense, I suppose in that sense I should've realized I was answering my question when I said that I tried to read up on the question and couldn't find much! <span aria-label=\"sweat smile\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f605\" role=\"img\" title=\"sweat smile\">:sweat_smile:</span> Thanks for the speedy reply!</p>\n<p>One of my goals is to also have accept/reject verdict is entirely done in a theory without nested/mutual inductives, to try to simplify+harden the logic. (I think this similar to lean-ind-models but I'm trying not to look at/read about it too much to try to get a maximally different kernel.) I can lower it when the universes are homogeneous, which got me thinking about the heterogeneous case and if that was a necessary condition.</p>\n<p>I <em>think</em> I might have a construction that lowers it for the heterogeneous case, which would feel (to me) like a good reason to make it safe. If I can flesh that out properly, I suppose that in principle it could be added to the Lean elaborator to do a similar lowering there on heterogeneous mutual blocks...?</p>",
        "id": 619326443,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Meiburg",
        "timestamp": 1787760847
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"448405\">Alex Meiburg</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113488-general/topic/Lean.20Kernel.20Arena/near/619326443\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>If I can flesh that out properly, I suppose that in principle it could be added to the Lean elaborator to do a similar lowering there on heterogeneous mutual blocks...?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes. But the qualifier \"in principle\" is doing a lot of work here: in practice the kernel will not be changed just because the change is more permissive and known to be consistent. It would require a good reason to modify the kernel.</p>",
        "id": 619328059,
        "sender_full_name": "Floris van Doorn",
        "timestamp": 1787761279
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Certainly! I don't think this would merit kernel changes by any means. I mean a command elaborator that expands mutual (or maybe you want a new <code>mutual_multiple_univ</code> or something) that does this at elaboration time. I have less of a sense of what the barrier to being \"worth it\" there is.</p>",
        "id": 619330235,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Meiburg",
        "timestamp": 1787761841
    },
    {
